{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DWK2mkz5BKjfsbgVjEJALx","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"## Literature Map — Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity\\\\n## 增量扫描: 2026-05-14\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 已知文献（baseline）\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: spark-aaai2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SparK: Query-Aware Unstructured Sparsity with Recoverable KV Cache Channel Pruning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: AAAI 2026\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance: 首篇报告 compound sparsity 总是 super-additive（ThinK+SnapKV 退化 65%），核心对标\\\\n  key_finding: compound KV cache sparsity (channel + token) 产生 super-additive degradation\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: thinkv-iclr2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"ThinKV\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2026 Oral\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: phase 划分思想来源，但只做单一压缩策略\\\\n  key_finding: KV cache 可按 phase 划分应用不同策略\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: rac-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"RAC\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: reasoning model 对 sparsity 更敏感，支撑评估任务选择\\\\n  key_finding: reasoning model 对 sparsity 敏感度高于一般 LLM\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: raas-acl2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"RaaS\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: milestone tokens 先重要后衰减，支撑 phase-dependent importance\\\\n  key_finding: token importance 随 decoding phase 非单调变化\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: mixeddimkv-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"MixedDimKV\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: multi-dimension compression budget allocation，与 joint controller 思路平行\\\\n  key_finding: 多维度压缩 budget allocation 优于均匀分配\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 新增 — Compound / Joint Compression Interaction\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: slim-icml2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SLiM: One-shot Quantization and Sparsity with Low-rank Approximation for LLM Weight Compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Mozaffari et al.\\\\n  venue: ICML 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: 首个正式处理 compound compression error 的框架——用 low-rank adapter 补偿 Q+S 聚合误差，证明误差非加性\\\\n  key_finding: quantization + sparsity 聚合误差非加性；invertible saliency function 精确计算 low-rank 补偿\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: unicomp-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"UniComp: A Unified Evaluation of Large Language Model Compression via Pruning, Quantization and Distillation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Wang et al.\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2602.09130\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  relevance: 40+ benchmarks 系统评估 compound compression，发现 compression 有 consistent knowledge bias\\\\n  key_finding: compression 对 knowledge-intensive tasks 保留好但 reasoning/multilingual 显著退化；quantization 整体 trade-off 最佳\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: compression-ordering-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Systematic Study of Compression Ordering for Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2511.19495\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 证明 compound compression ordering 显著影响结果，early quantization 造成不可逆信息损失\\\\n  key_finding: P→KD→Q 最优；quantization first 产生不可逆退化；interaction 依赖 ordering\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: phase-transition-compression-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Phase Transitions in Large Language Model Compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Ma et al.\\\\n  venue: npj Artificial Intelligence (Nature), 2026\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 直接支撑 phase-gating 理论动机——不同压缩维度有独立 phase transition point，联合安全区可叠加\\\\n  key_finding: structured pruning 30-45% 崩溃，unstructured 55-65%；多维度 PTP 正交，联合安全区可实现 10% 压缩\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: graspune-acl2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GRASPrune: Global Gating for Budgeted Structured Pruning of Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Wang et al.\\\\n  venue: ACL 2026 Main\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance: 最直接 competitor——联合 pruning FFN channels + KV head groups with learned gate scores under global budget\\\\n  key_finding: learned gate + projected STE 全局 budget 下联合 prune FFN+heads，高压缩比下显著优于逐层独立 pruning\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: mustafar-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"MUSTAFAR: Promoting Unstructured Sparsity for KV Cache Pruning in LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Joo et al.\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: per-token magnitude pruning 70% sparsity 无损，与 token eviction 可组合——potential compound baseline\\\\n  key_finding: unstructured KV sparsity (per-token magnitude) 70% 无损，bitmap 格式 2.23x throughput\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 新增 — Non-monotonic / Sign Reversal / Phase Effects\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: decision-repr-transition-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Understanding Performance Collapse in Layer-Pruned LLMs via Decision Representation Transitions\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2605.07271\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: layer pruning 存在 Silent/Decisive Phase 二分——pruning 敏感度 phase-dependent，直接支撑 phase-gated 思想\\\\n  key_finding: Decision Margin 在 Transition Point 发生符号反转（负→正）；pruning Silent Phase 崩溃，Decisive Phase 可安全 prune\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: doing-more-with-less-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Doing More With Less: Revisiting the Effectiveness of LLM Pruning for Test-Time Scaling\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2604.25098\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 符号反转直接证据——unstructured pruning + TTS 可超越未剪枝模型\\\\n  key_finding: unstructured pruning + test-time scaling 可超越 unpruned baseline（Qwen3-8B）；structured pruning 严重退化\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: sparse-frontier-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Sparse Frontier: Sparse Attention Trade-offs in Transformer LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2504.17768\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  relevance: 最大规模 sparse attention 评估，无通用最优策略，trade-off 依赖 task 和 phase\\\\n  key_finding: decoding vs prefilling 可承受 sparsity 不同；大模型+高稀疏可优于小模型+密集\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 新增 — Adaptive / Learned Controllers\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: obcache-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"OBCache: Optimal Brain KV Cache Pruning for Efficient Long-Context LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2510.07651\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: OBD 理论推导 joint K-V saliency score，可作为 importance-based masking 的理论参照\\\\n  key_finding: 二阶 Taylor 近似推导 joint K-V pruning score；key-pruning 比 value-pruning 影响更大\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: pagedeviction-eacl2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"PagedEviction: Structured Block-wise KV Cache Pruning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: EACL 2026 Findings\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: prefill/decode 两阶段不同 eviction 策略，呼应 phase-gated 思想\\\\n  key_finding: block-wise eviction prefill/decode 分阶段执行，优于 token-level baselines\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: adakv-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Ada-KV: Adaptive Budget Allocation for KV Cache Eviction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: per-head adaptive budget 分配，与 per-phase budget allocation 正交互补\\\\n  key_finding: 自适应 per-head KV cache budget 优于均匀分配\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: sol-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SOL: Self-Optimizing Language Models — Compute Where it Counts\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2605.10875\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: learned policy 按 token difficulty 分配 compute，与 learned joint controller 方法论平行\\\\n  key_finding: 学习 per-token compute allocation policy，easy tokens 少算 hard tokens 多算\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 新增 — Phase-Adaptive Dynamic Compression (2026-05-14 scan)\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: twilight-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Twilight: Adaptive Attention Sparsity with Hierarchical Top-p Pruning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Lin, Tang, Yang, Wang, Tang, Tian, Stoica, Han, Gao\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: 证明 fixed budget 次优，per-step adaptive budget 更优；但单轴无 compound interaction\\\\n  key_finding: top-p 采样思想做 per-step adaptive attention budget，最多 prune 98% tokens\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lava-emnlp2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"LAVa: Layer-wise KV Cache Eviction with Dynamic Budget Allocation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Shen, Yuan, Zhang, Wang, Jiang, Nguyen\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2025 Findings\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: layer budgets 对 generation 重要、head budgets 对 extraction 重要——支撑 task-conditional budget\\\\n  key_finding: 基于 Layer Attention Output Loss 的 unified eviction + dynamic budget；budget 需求 task-dependent\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lkv-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"LKV: End-to-End Learning of Head-wise Budgets and Token Selection for LLM KV Cache Eviction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2605.06676\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: 首个端到端可微 budget+selection 联合学习框架；可作 phase-gated controller backbone\\\\n  key_finding: LKV-H 学 head budget + LKV-T 学 token selection，全面超越启发式方法\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: defensivekv-iclr2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DefensiveKV: Taming the Fragility of KV Cache Eviction in LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2026\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance: 独立发现 importance-based eviction 的脆弱性——stability assumption 不成立，importance 高度非稳态\\\\n  key_finding: mean aggregation 在极端情况下 vulnerable；defensive aggregation 控制 worst-case risk，2.3-4.3x 质量损失降低\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: retentivekv-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"RetentiveKV: State-Space Memory for Uncertainty-Aware Multimodal KV Cache Eviction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2605.04075\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: \\\\\\\"Deferred Importance\\\\\\\" 现象——token 初始 salience 低但后期变 pivotal，直接支撑 crossover 机制\\\\n  key_finding: 视觉 tokens 存在 deferred importance——早期不重要晚期关键；单一时刻 importance 评估不可靠\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: pitfalls-kvcache-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Pitfalls of KV Cache Compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2510.00231\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: multi-instruction 场景下部分指令被压缩严重忽略——condition-dependent failure\\\\n  key_finding: 特定 instructions 在压缩下退化极快甚至完全被忽略；compression method + order 产生 bias 交互\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: greedy-bias-kvcache-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"More Than a Quick Glance: Overcoming the Greedy Bias in KV-Cache Compression\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2602.02199\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 单一 attention-score metric 是 greedy 的——miss structurally relevant tokens\\\\n  key_finding: 单 metric 有 greedy bias；multi-metric scoring 克服 condition-dependent metric failure\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: thinking-tokens-mi-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Demystifying Reasoning Dynamics with Mutual Information: Thinking Tokens are Information Peaks\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: reasoning 中 MI 在 thinking tokens 处突然 peak——compression 需避开 info-critical phases\\\\n  key_finding: thinking tokens (Hmm/Wait/Therefore) 处 MI 突增；这些 tokens 对推理性能不成比例重要\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lserve-mlsys2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"LServe: Efficient Long-sequence LLM Serving with Unified Sparse Attention\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: MLSys 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: sparse attention + KV quantization 工业级联合部署但无 interaction 分析\\\\n  key_finding: 统一 static/dynamic sparsity + KV quant 到单 GPU kernel；prefilling 2.9x 加速\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: squeezeattention-iclr2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SqueezeAttention: 2D Management of KV-Cache via Layer-wise Optimal Budget\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: layer × sequence 二维 budget 优化；我们增加第三维——temporal phase\\\\n  key_finding: layer-wise cosine similarity 区分 layer 重要性，30-70% memory reduction\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nsa-acl2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"NSA: Native Sparse Attention\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: DeepSeek\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Best Paper\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: hardware-aligned trainable sparse attention；hierarchical 策略（coarse compression + fine selection）呼应 multi-phase\\\\n  key_finding: 端到端可训练 sparse attention，64k 解码 11.6x 加速；coarse+fine 分层策略\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: evicpress-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"EVICPRESS: Joint KV-Cache Compression and Eviction for Efficient LLM Serving\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2512.14946\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: 联合 eviction + quantization 优化 KV cache——compound 方向但仅 KV 内部\\\\n  key_finding: joint eviction + quantization 优于独立应用；但无 head pruning compound\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: phase-transition-pruning-thermo-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Phase Transitions in Neural Networks Pruning (Thermodynamic Analysis)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2602.15224\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 自由能分析预测 sharp collapse；transition 前稳定 3-5%，跨越后 30%+ 骤降\\\\n  key_finding: free-energy phase transition 预测 pruning 崩溃点；importance 方法可能在某 phase 跨越 PTP\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: token-budget-aware-acl2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Token-Budget-Aware LLM Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: reasoning budget 应按 query 难度自适应分配——支撑 phase-adaptive compression\\\\n  key_finding: self-consistency 作为 thinking necessity proxy 动态调整 reasoning token budget\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 新增 — C5b Negative Result 文献联动 (2026-05-14)\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: physics-kvcache-2026\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Understanding the Physics of Key-Value Cache Compression for LLMs through Attention Dynamics\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: arXiv 2603.01426\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  relevance: 直接解释 C5b negative——representational rigidity 使 budget redistribution 无法修复 routing collapse；90% 附近存在 sharp hallucination cliff (phase transition)\\\\n  key_finding: head-level consensus 过度导致 representational rigidity，token 存活但 routing flexibility 崩溃；compression 存在 sharp safety cliff 对应 semantic reachability phase transition\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: rkv-neurips2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"R-KV: Redundancy-aware KV Cache Compression for Reasoning Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: Cai et al.\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  relevance: reasoning model 专用单轴 KV compression，10% cache 保留 ~100% 性能；但不涉及 compound interaction，与 C5b failure 机制不直接相关\\\\n  key_finding: importance + non-redundancy 双准则 token ranking，10% KV cache 保留全精度性能；单轴方法无 compound 讨论\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 13069, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TedWEbi2Pdo2JuREeanJ5M","session_id":"f46d8a0d-9101-4daf-83a4-2cd94cb2633d","uuid":"6f2dcd9a-e4ae-45c0-8ae0-d85da868b3d7","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:05:43.259Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ujf1Q9d1Pf7LxW3R657jJ3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Benjamini-Hochberg FDR Correction}\n2\t\\label{app:bh-fdr}\n3\t\n4\tTable~\\ref{tab:bh-fdr} compares Bonferroni ($k{=}4$, per model$\\times$strategy family) and Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction across all 44 statistical tests. BH-FDR recovers 5 additional significant results beyond Bonferroni, all in low-effect-size conditions (random eviction or cross-training-paradigm random comparisons). All 32 Bonferroni-significant results remain significant under BH-FDR.\n5\t\n6\t\\begin{table}[t]\n7\t\\centering\n8\t\\small\n9\t\\caption{Bonferroni vs.\\ BH-FDR correction summary ($\\alpha{=}0.05$, 44 tests). ``BH only'' denotes tests significant under BH-FDR but not Bonferroni.}\n10\t\\label{tab:bh-fdr}\n11\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n12\t\\toprule\n13\tCategory & Bonf.\\ sig. & BH-only sig. \\\\\n14\t\\midrule\n15\tPer-condition Wilcoxon ($k{=}16$) & 10 & 2 \\\\\n16\tImp vs.\\ rand ($k{=}8$) & 8 & 0 \\\\\n17\tAdjacent sparsity ($k{=}12$) & 10 & 1 \\\\\n18\tCross-paradigm Mann-Whitney ($k{=}8$) & 4 & 2 \\\\\n19\t\\midrule\n20\tTotal ($k{=}44$) & 32 & 5 \\\\\n21\t\\bottomrule\n22\t\\end{tabular}\n23\t\\end{table}\n24\t\n25\tThe 5 BH-only results are: DeepSeek random $s{=}0.25$ and $s{=}0.30$ per-condition Wilcoxon ($p_{\\text{Bonf}}{=}0.064$/$0.065$, BH $q{=}0.002$), DeepSeek random $s{=}0.25 \\to 0.30$ adjacent-sparsity step ($p_{\\text{Bonf}}{=}0.078$, BH $q{=}0.002$), and cross-training-paradigm random $s{=}0.25$/$s{=}0.30$ Mann-Whitney ($p_{\\text{Bonf}}{=}0.66$/$0.17$, BH $q{=}0.018$/$0.005$). All involve random eviction---the weakest effects---indicating that Bonferroni is conservative but does not miss any importance-based findings.\n26\t\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Minimum Phase Length Sensitivity}\n29\t\\label{app:mpl-sensitivity}\n30\t\n31\tTable~\\ref{tab:mpl-sensitivity} reports the paired median difference $\\Delta$ under three \\texttt{min\\_phase\\_length} (mpl) settings across all 16 conditions (2 models $\\times$ 2 strategies $\\times$ 4 sparsity levels). Conclusions are robust: all importance-based conditions remain significant at $p_{\\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$ across mpl $\\in \\{5, 10, 20\\}$; random conditions maintain the same significance pattern (non-significant at $s \\leq 0.20$, significant at $s \\geq 0.25$). Median $\\Delta$ values vary by $<5\\%$ between mpl settings for DeepSeek importance, supporting that the severity gradient is not an artifact of the merging threshold.\n32\t\n33\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n34\t\\centering\n35\t\\small\n36\t\\caption{Sensitivity of median $\\Delta$ to \\texttt{min\\_phase\\_length} (mpl). $n_{\\text{eff}}$: effective sample size after exclusions. All importance-based $p_{\\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$; ``ns'' denotes $p_{\\text{Bonf}} > 0.05$.}\n37\t\\label{tab:mpl-sensitivity}\n38\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrrr@{}}\n39\t\\toprule\n40\tModel & Strategy & $s$ & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{mpl$=$5} & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{mpl$=$10} & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{mpl$=$20} & $n_{\\text{eff}}$(10) & $n_{\\text{eff}}$(20) \\\\\n41\t\\midrule\n42\tDS & Imp & 0.15 & 0.391 & 0.391 & 0.393 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n43\tDS & Imp & 0.20 & 1.215 & 1.215 & 1.235 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n44\tDS & Imp & 0.25 & 3.986 & 3.986 & 4.017 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n45\tDS & Imp & 0.30 & 8.481 & 8.481 & 8.420 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n46\t\\addlinespace\n47\tDS & Rand & 0.15 & 0.014 & 0.014\\textsuperscript{ns} & 0.012\\textsuperscript{ns} & 496 & 433 \\\\\n48\tDS & Rand & 0.20 & 0.031\\textsuperscript{ns} & 0.031\\textsuperscript{ns} & 0.023\\textsuperscript{ns} & 496 & 433 \\\\\n49\tDS & Rand & 0.25 & 0.257 & 0.257 & 0.190 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n50\tDS & Rand & 0.30 & 0.510 & 0.510 & 0.410 & 496 & 433 \\\\\n51\t\\addlinespace\n52\tQW & Imp & 0.15 & 0.066 & 0.066 & 0.066 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n53\tQW & Imp & 0.20 & 0.307 & 0.307 & 0.325 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n54\tQW & Imp & 0.25 & 1.074 & 1.074 & 1.106 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n55\tQW & Imp & 0.30 & 3.155 & 3.155 & 3.581 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n56\t\\addlinespace\n57\tQW & Rand & 0.15 & $-$0.011\\textsuperscript{ns} & $-$0.011\\textsuperscript{ns} & $-$0.011\\textsuperscript{ns} & 475 & 289 \\\\\n58\tQW & Rand & 0.20 & 0.015\\textsuperscript{ns} & 0.015\\textsuperscript{ns} & 0.015\\textsuperscript{ns} & 475 & 289 \\\\\n59\tQW & Rand & 0.25 & 0.393 & 0.393 & 0.266 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n60\tQW & Rand & 0.30 & 0.913 & 0.913 & 0.788 & 475 & 289 \\\\\n61\t\\bottomrule\n62\t\\end{tabular}\n63\t\\end{table*}\n64\t\n65\tThe main effect of increasing mpl is reduced $n_{\\text{eff}}$: at mpl$=$20, DeepSeek retains 433/500 problems and Qwen retains 289/500 (mid-null rate rises to 71.2\\% and 84.6\\%, respectively). Despite this sample reduction, all significance conclusions hold.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t\\section{Segmentation Robustness}\n69\t\\label{app:seg-robust}\n70\t\n71\tTable~\\ref{tab:seg-robust} reports the paired median difference $\\Delta$ under tercile, quartile, and quintile segmentation for both models under importance-based eviction. Phase gradient direction and statistical significance are preserved under quartile and quintile segmentation ($n{=}50$ spot check), indicating that the tercile-based findings are not an artifact of the segmentation choice.\n72\t\n73\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n74\t\\centering\n75\t\\small\n76\t\\caption{Segmentation robustness: median $\\Delta$ under tercile ($n{=}496$/$475$), quartile ($n{=}50$ spot check), and quintile ($n{=}50$ spot check) segmentation. Importance-based eviction; all $p_{\\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-7}$.}\n77\t\\label{tab:seg-robust}\n78\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n79\t\\toprule\n80\tModel & $s$ & Tercile & Quartile\\; [95\\% CI] & Quintile\\; [95\\% CI] \\\\\n81\t\\midrule\n82\t\\multirow{4}{*}{DeepSeek} & 0.15 & 0.391 & 0.491\\; $[0.44,\\; 0.70]$ & 0.505\\; $[0.33,\\; 0.71]$ \\\\\n83\t& 0.20 & 1.215 & 1.388\\; $[1.05,\\; 1.79]$ & 1.197\\; $[1.02,\\; 1.76]$ \\\\\n84\t& 0.25 & 3.986 & 4.322\\; $[3.62,\\; 8.06]$ & 4.960\\; $[3.80,\\; 9.02]$ \\\\\n85\t& 0.30 & 8.481 & 12.930\\; $[9.83,\\; 19.10]$ & 11.548\\; $[10.06,\\; 18.51]$ \\\\\n86\t\\addlinespace\n87\t\\multirow{4}{*}{Qwen} & 0.15 & 0.066 & 0.226\\; $[0.14,\\; 0.27]$ & 0.203\\; $[0.13,\\; 0.26]$ \\\\\n88\t& 0.20 & 0.307 & 0.561\\; $[0.35,\\; 0.77]$ & 0.517\\; $[0.29,\\; 0.75]$ \\\\\n89\t& 0.25 & 1.074 & 1.870\\; $[1.05,\\; 2.44]$ & 1.801\\; $[1.13,\\; 2.38]$ \\\\\n90\t& 0.30 & 3.155 & 4.097\\; $[2.91,\\; 4.94]$ & 4.009\\; $[3.28,\\; 5.34]$ \\\\\n91\t\\bottomrule\n92\t\\end{tabular}\n93\t\\end{table*}\n94\t\n95\tThe quartile and quintile $\\Delta$ values are comparable to or slightly larger than tercile values because finer-grained segmentation isolates more extreme phases (the ``lowest'' quintile captures a more concentrated low-sparsity segment than the lowest tercile). The monotonic dose-response ($\\Delta$ increasing with $s$) is preserved across all three segmentation schemes for both models, supporting that the severity gradient is not an artifact of the tercile segmentation choice.\n96\t\n97\t\n98\t\\section{Robustness to Asymmetric Sparsity Allocation}\n99\t\\label{app:asymmetric-robustness}\n100\t\n101\tOur main experiments use symmetric sparsity ($s_{\\text{head}} = s_{\\text{KV}} = s$). To verify that the phase-gated severity gradient does not depend on this symmetric assumption, we evaluate two asymmetric configurations at a fixed total sparsity budget equivalent to $s{=}0.25$, using importance-based eviction on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B.\n102\t\n103\t\\begin{table}[h]\n104\t\\centering\n105\t\\small\n106\t\\caption{Asymmetric sparsity robustness: per-phase II under two head/KV allocations (DeepSeek, importance-based eviction, $n{=}50$ spot check).}\n107\t\\label{tab:asymmetric-robust}\n108\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n109\t\\toprule\n110\tConfig & $s_{\\text{head}}$ & $s_{\\text{KV}}$ & $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{II}}_{\\textit{low}}$ & $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{II}}_{\\textit{mid}}$ & $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{II}}_{\\textit{high}}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n111\t\\midrule\n112\tA (KV-heavy) & 0.20 & 0.30 & 19.51 & 6.05 & 0.38 & $+$19.13 \\\\\n113\tB (head-heavy) & 0.30 & 0.20 & 18.77 & 3.97 & 0.52 & $+$18.25 \\\\\n114\t\\addlinespace\n115\tSymmetric & 0.25 & 0.25 & 3.97 & --- & 0.02 & $+$3.99 \\\\\n116\t\\bottomrule\n117\t\\end{tabular}\n118\t\\end{table}\n119\t\n120\tBoth asymmetric configurations produce large positive $\\Delta$ ($+19.13$ and $+18.25$), supporting that the phase-gated severity gradient is robust to the head/KV allocation ratio. The KV-heavy configuration (A) exhibits higher mid-phase II ($6.05$ vs.\\ $3.97$), suggesting that the KV eviction axis has a stronger impact on intermediate reasoning phases. Both asymmetric $\\Delta$ values exceed the symmetric baseline ($3.99$), consistent with one axis operating at $s{=}0.30$---above the symmetric maximum tested---which amplifies overall severity. A systematic investigation of the full asymmetric interaction surface is left for future work.\n121\t\n122\t\n123\t\\section{Outlier Robustness Analysis}\n124\t\\label{app:outlier-robustness}\n125\t\n126\tThe extreme mean/median ratio (up to $97{,}640{:}1$ in individual conditions) reflects heavy-tailed II distributions driven by near-zero denominators in specific problems. We verify robustness via symmetric winsorization at $5\\%$ and $10\\%$ levels. All median-based statistics---paired median difference $\\Delta$, Wilcoxon signed-rank $p$-values, and amplification ratio $G$---are strictly invariant under winsorization, as expected for rank-based estimators. The five core claims---compound exceeds individual, importance exceeds random, monotonic dose-response, $G \\gg 1$ for importance (auxiliary illustration of phase concentration), $G \\approx 1$ for random---are fully robust. The extreme outlier severity further validates our choice of median-based inference over mean-based alternatives. $G$ values in this robustness analysis follow the ratio-of-medians convention (Table~\\ref{tab:profiling-subset}); the primary $G$ definition (Eq.~\\ref{eq:gradient}) is similarly invariant as a median of per-problem ratios.\n127\t\n128\t\n129\t\\section{\\texttt{<think>} Token Phase Validation}\n130\t\\label{app:think-validation}\n131\t\n132\tDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B generates explicit \\texttt{<think>}...\\texttt{</think>} delimiters that demarcate chain-of-thought reasoning from final answers, providing a model-generated ground truth for reasoning phase boundaries. We evaluate whether our attention-sparsity-based phase segmentation (\\S\\ref{par:phase-segmentation}) recovers these boundaries on GSM8K ($n{=}48$ problems with valid phase assignments), using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation rather than a fixed global threshold.\n133\t\n134\t\\paragraph{Naming convention.}\n135\tA note on terminology: DeepSeek's \\texttt{<think>} phase (active reasoning) exhibits \\emph{low} attention sparsity---attention is broadly distributed across many positions during exploratory computation. Conversely, the answer phase exhibits \\emph{high} attention sparsity---attention concentrates on a few positions during structured output. Thus PACS ``low-sparsity phase'' corresponds to the model's thinking phase, and ``high-sparsity phase'' to the answer phase. The physical phenomenon is consistent: broadly distributed attention characterizes reasoning in both frameworks.\n136\t\n137\t\\paragraph{Token-level agreement.}\n138\tWe binarize each token as ``thinking'' (inside \\texttt{<think>}) or ``answer'' (outside) and compare against the sparsity-based phase assignment. The adaptive segmentation achieves substantial agreement: $\\kappa{=}0.88$ (per-problem mean $0.85$, median $0.88$), with token-level accuracy of $94.2\\%$. Per-problem AUC-ROC is $0.98$ (median $0.99$), indicating near-perfect discriminability between thinking and answer phases based on attention sparsity alone. Of problems, $75\\%$ achieve $\\kappa \\geq 0.80$ and $100\\%$ achieve $\\kappa \\geq 0.60$. The \\texttt{<think>} phase exhibits significantly lower attention sparsity than the answer phase ($0.656$ vs.\\ $0.763$; Cohen's $d{=}-0.80$), indicating that attention distribution breadth reliably distinguishes reasoning from structured computation.\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Boundary proximity.}\n141\tThe median distance between the sparsity-based phase boundary and the nearest \\texttt{<think>}/\\texttt{</think>} delimiter is 1 token, with $81.2\\%$ of boundaries falling within 10 tokens. This tight alignment indicates that the attention sparsity transition closely tracks the model's self-identified shift from exploratory reasoning to structured answer generation.\n142\t\n143\t\\paragraph{Phase composition and II correlation.}\n144\tWe examine whether the fraction of thinking tokens within each phase correlates with compound severity (II). At the mid-phase level, the thinking-token fraction correlates with II (Spearman $\\rho{=}0.49$, $p < 0.001$), consistent with mid-phase segments that contain more reasoning content being more vulnerable to compound sparsity. Low- and high-phase correlations are non-significant, as expected: these phases are compositionally dominated by thinking or answer tokens, respectively, leaving insufficient variance for correlation.\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{MATH-500 indirect validation.}\n147\tDirect $\\kappa$ validation requires explicit \\texttt{<think>} delimiters, available only on GSM8K. On MATH-500 ($n{=}500$), we instead assess \\emph{temporal ordering consistency}: the fraction of problems where the low-sparsity phase precedes the high-sparsity phase, consistent with a thinking-then-answering generation structure. DeepSeek achieves a temporal ordering score of $0.576$ with $100\\%$ of problems exhibiting the expected direction (low-sparsity before high-sparsity). Qwen, which lacks \\texttt{<think>} delimiters, shows weaker but directionally consistent temporal ordering ($0.479$). This indirect evidence supports the validity of sparsity-based phase segmentation on MATH-500 but does not constitute direct $\\kappa$ validation.\n148\t\n149\t\\paragraph{Scope.}\n150\tThe direct $\\kappa$ validation ($\\kappa{=}0.88$, $n{=}48$) is limited to DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on GSM8K; MATH-500 has only indirect (temporal ordering) validation. Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not produce explicit reasoning delimiters, precluding direct $\\kappa$ validation on either benchmark. Phase segmentation is computed on the dense model's attention patterns and is independent of sparsity level, so the agreement statistics are reported once rather than per-$s$.\n151\t\n152\t\n153\t\\subsection{Non-Think Token Quality Under Compound Sparsity}\n154\t\\label{app:nt-samples}\n155\t\n156\tTokens generated outside \\texttt{<think>}...\\texttt{</think>} delimiters---i.e., the final answer tokens---fall predominantly in the high-sparsity attention phase, where compound interaction is minimal (\\S\\ref{sec:answer-region}: 93.2\\% of \\verb|\\boxed{}| markers reside in the high-sparsity phase). We illustrate the differential impact of compound sparsity on non-think (answer) versus think (reasoning) tokens with two representative examples from the GSM8K validation set (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, $s{=}0.25$, importance-based eviction).\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Example 1: Preserved answer, degraded reasoning.}\n159\tUnder compound sparsity, the non-think answer tokens (\\texttt{The answer is 42}) are generated with near-baseline perplexity (PPL ratio $1.2{\\times}$), while the preceding think-phase tokens exhibit $4.8{\\times}$ PPL inflation. The final answer remains correct despite substantial reasoning-trace degradation---a ``silent reasoning error'' scenario where intermediate logic is corrupted but the output format masks the damage.\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{Example 2: Cascading failure at high sparsity.}\n162\tAt $s{=}0.30$, reasoning-phase PPL inflation ($11.3{\\times}$) propagates into the answer phase: the model produces a syntactically valid but numerically incorrect \\verb|\\boxed{}| output (PPL ratio $3.1{\\times}$ on answer tokens vs.\\ $1.1{\\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$). This illustrates the sparsity threshold beyond which compound severity breaches the phase boundary, consistent with the accuracy cliff observed for Qwen at $s{=}0.30$ ($\\delta{=}-27$\\,pp; Table~\\ref{tab:accuracy}).\n163\t\n164\t\\paragraph{Implication.}\n165\tThese examples illustrate that pruning-induced compound severity is phase-selective: non-think tokens are relatively protected at moderate sparsity ($s \\leq 0.25$) because they coincide with the high-sparsity attention phase where importance scores retain discriminative power. At higher sparsity ($s{=}0.30$), discrimination collapse (\\S\\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}) erodes this protection, and compound severity spills into the answer phase.\n166\t\n167\t\n168\t\\section{Amplification Ratio ($G$) Details}\n169\t\\label{app:g-metric}\n170\t\n171\tThe per-problem amplification ratio $G^{(i)} = \\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\textit{low}} / \\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\textit{high}}$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:gradient}) provides an auxiliary illustrative perspective on the primary metric $\\Delta$. Table~\\ref{tab:g-metric} reports $G$ with bootstrap 95\\% CIs for all conditions. $G$ serves as a visualization aid; primary statistical evidence for the phase-modulated severity gradient is provided by $\\Delta$ and the associated Wilcoxon tests (\\S\\ref{sec:phase-amplification}).\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\small\n176\t\\caption{Per-problem amplification ratio $G$ (median of $G^{(i)}$) with bootstrap 95\\% CIs ($B{=}10{,}000$). $G$ serves as an auxiliary visualization of phase concentration; primary statistical evidence for the severity gradient is provided by the Interaction Index $\\Delta$ (\\S\\ref{sec:phase-amplification}). $G \\gg 1$ suggests phase-concentrated severity; $G \\approx 1$ suggests uniform severity across phases.}\n177\t\\label{tab:g-metric}\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\t$s$ & Strategy & $G$\\; [95\\% CI] (DeepSeek) & $G$\\; [95\\% CI] (Qwen) \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\t0.15 & Importance & $\\mathbf{7.5}$\\; $[4.1,\\; 11.4]$ & n.d.$^{\\dagger}$ \\\\\n183\t0.15 & Random     & 0.5\\; $[0.4,\\; 0.6]$ & 0.7\\; $[0.4,\\; 0.8]$ \\\\\n184\t\\addlinespace\n185\t0.20 & Importance & $\\mathbf{17.9}$\\; $[12.7,\\; 26.3]$ & $\\mathbf{2.6}$\\; $[0.5,\\; 5.1]$ \\\\\n186\t0.20 & Random     & 0.8\\; $[0.6,\\; 0.9]$ & 0.9\\; $[0.7,\\; 1.0]$ \\\\\n187\t\\addlinespace\n188\t0.25 & Importance & $\\mathbf{49.1}$\\; $[40.1,\\; 57.8]$ & $\\mathbf{9.5}$\\; $[5.2,\\; 14.8]$ \\\\\n189\t0.25 & Random     & 1.1\\; $[1.0,\\; 1.2]$ & 1.3\\; $[1.2,\\; 1.4]$ \\\\\n190\t\\addlinespace\n191\t0.30 & Importance & $\\mathbf{79.8}$\\; $[63.3,\\; 96.5]$ & $\\mathbf{36.9}$\\; $[25.3,\\; 49.1]$ \\\\\n192\t0.30 & Random     & 1.1\\; $[1.0,\\; 1.3]$ & 1.3\\; $[1.2,\\; 1.4]$ \\\\\n193\t\\bottomrule\n194\t\\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{0.85\\textwidth}}{\\footnotesize $^{\\dagger}$ $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{II}}_{\\textit{high}} \\approx 0$; ratio undefined ($G$ CI: $[-0.2, 0.9]$). $\\Delta{=}0.07$, $p{=}8.7 \\times 10^{-15}$ supports $\\mathrm{II}_{\\textit{low}} > \\mathrm{II}_{\\textit{high}}$.}\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table*}\n197\t\n198\tThe $G$ values illustrate that phase-concentrated severity is associated with importance-based eviction ($G{=}7.5$--$79.8{\\times}$ for DeepSeek, $2.6$--$36.9{\\times}$ for Qwen), while random eviction suggests weak modulation at $s \\geq 0.25$ ($G \\approx 1.1$--$1.3$; CIs exclude 1) and near-uniform severity at $s \\leq 0.20$ (CIs span or touch 1). The monotonic increase in $G$ with sparsity is consistent with the $\\Delta$ dose-response in Table~\\ref{tab:main-results}, but $G$ should be interpreted as a descriptive ratio rather than an independent statistical test---the formal evidence rests on the paired $\\Delta$ and associated Wilcoxon tests.\n199\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":